<p><a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/09/science/scientists-discover-the-earliest-evidence-of-drug-use-in-really-old-teeth.html?unlocked_article_code=1.AFE.knaI.cHXoX9CLft4i&amp;smid=url-share">“Scientists Discover the Earliest Evidence of Drug Use in Really Old Teeth”</a> may sound like odd headline language, at least by the traditional standards of the <em>New York</em>&nbsp;<em>Times</em>. But then, the teeth in question are, indeed, really old. “Archaeologists have pushed the timeline of human drug use back further than ever before by studying teeth that belonged to a hunter-gatherer who lived in Indonesia sometime from 16,000 to 25,000 years ago,” writes reporter Becky Ferreira. This beats the <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/traces-13000-year-old-beer-found-israel-180970282/">13,000-year-old beer from Israel</a> that previously held the title of oldest known mind-altering comestible. “The individual’s molars, which were found in a cave on the island of Sulawesi, are marked with deep grooves that were most likely left by incessant sucking on betel nuts, which release psychoactive stimulants.”</p>
<p>All these millennia later, betel nut-chewing remains a major ritual and pastime. Though it seems never to have caught on in the English-speaking world, it has a great many adherents in not just modern-day Indonesia <span style="font-size: 13pt;">but also other parts of southeast and south Asia, including India, as well as in China. </span></p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>Mixed into a compound with the leaves of its own plant, slaked lime, and a variety of other ingredients from coconut to menthol to cloves to tobacco — the regional variations are countless — betel nuts can be chewed all day long, and often are. Whether you call it <em>paan</em>, <em>buai</em>, <em>doma</em>, <em>ngangà</em>, or something else entirely,&nbsp;the stuff is “reputed to be the world’s fourth most popular psychoactive substance after alcohol, caffeine, and nicotine,” write the authors of <a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aei1901">a study on the Sulawesi teeth newly published in <em>Science Advances</em></a>.</p>
<p>Before you seek out the nearest areca palm to experience what all the fuss is about for yourself, know that the documented health effects of betel nut-chewing include cancer, cardiovascular disease, liver damage, diabetes, and infertility; even still-fertile chewers run the risk of causing&nbsp;birth defects. At the very least,&nbsp;those who pick up the habit also acquire a characteristic red tooth stain, which has been seen on nearly 5,000-year-old skeletons unearthed in southeast Asia. In&nbsp;the case of the skull from Sulawesi,&nbsp;it wasn’t the color of the teeth but their wear patterns that told the tale. Griffith University professor&nbsp;Adam Brumm, who led the study, hypothesizes a “negative feedback loop” in which betel nuts inflicted dental damage on these ancient peoples, but also offered the only means of relieving the pain of that damage. Whether now or&nbsp;25,000 years ago, if one substance seems to be both causing your problems and providing their solutions, it’s probably time to quit.</p>
